# **Alongside — A Founding Document**

*Build New Habits Ltd  |  22 Jun 2026  |  v5*

## **Before We Begin**

This is every messy bit of me.

My intention. How I want to be remembered. My best side. It’s what I’ve always tried to do — sometimes failed, but always tried. As a teacher I tried to listen before judging. I tried to absorb the frustrations of policies that didn’t serve people. I tried to support when the systems around us made it hard. I didn’t always get it right. But I tried.

Alongside is that same intention, finally in a form that scales. It’s me in all of the products — Move, Rest, Learn, Love, every app in the family. The same philosophy. The same commitment. The same belief that people do better when they are genuinely understood.

This document exists so that every decision made about this product — every coach line, every design choice, every feature built or deliberately not built — can be tested against that intention.

If it doesn’t serve the person. If it doesn’t hear them. If it treats them like a radiator in a room.

It doesn’t belong here.

## **1. Why This Exists**

For a long time, I was the person fitness apps were built for.

I was running regularly. Playing tennis. Playing football. Probably doing too much, but I loved it — I was active and engaged and feeling genuinely good in my body. Then I hurt my glutes, my hamstring, and my lower back. The injury triggered sciatic issues and running became impossible. I tried to keep exercising through it, but almost every movement required muscles that were now doing something they couldn’t do. Some days I couldn’t walk far. I needed to stretch instead of rest. I needed help navigating something complicated and specific.

So I opened fitness apps.

They didn’t even ask whether I carried an injury. They didn’t ask what my energy levels were like, or what I was dealing with, or what had brought me to this particular moment. The apps assumed I was starting from zero with nothing wrong and with unlimited motivation. There were options inside them, but using those options required me to already know how to navigate my own recovery — which was precisely what I needed help with. The expertise was locked inside the tool and inaccessible to the person who needed it most.

What I needed wasn’t a programme. It was a moment of being understood. Something that could say: *I can see this is hard. Let’s find a way through it together — one that looks after your movement and your wellbeing, because the two are not separate things.*

Instead, when I used fitness apps and approaches during that period, I went through the experience in stages. Motivated first, because I genuinely wanted to keep moving. Then frustrated, because the tools available kept failing me. Then disappointed, in myself as much as in the apps. Then sad — the particular sadness of losing something you loved. Then I just gave up.

I found nothing suitable. So I built Alongside.

## **2. Who It’s For**

There is a person who opens fitness apps and closes them again quietly.

Not because they don’t want to move. Not because they lack commitment or discipline or desire. Because the app doesn’t know they exist. It was built for someone else — someone with consistent energy, a cooperative body, predictable motivation, and the time and headspace to follow a rigid programme. That person is real. But they are not the majority.

The majority are people who feel, in the context of mainstream fitness culture, like a radiator in a room. Present. Functional. Completely unacknowledged until something goes wrong.

They are people with ADHD who built a habit carefully and then lost it when life became complicated, and absorbed that loss as personal failure rather than design failure. They are people with chronic pain who opened a workout app and found nothing that acknowledged their reality. They are people with anxiety who discovered that streak mechanics and progress rings created more pressure than they could carry. They are people who tried and failed and tried and failed and eventually — after going through the full sequence of motivated, frustrated, disappointed, sad, gave up — concluded that exercise simply wasn’t for them. When the truth was that the tools weren’t for them.

They are people who have burned out. Not just tired — the specific, accumulated exhaustion of someone who has been giving more than they’re receiving for longer than their nervous system could sustain. Who gets to the end of a day and has nothing left. Who finds that even the things that used to restore them have stopped working. Who looks fine from the outside and feels increasingly impossible from the inside. The fitness industry has almost nothing to offer this person. Most of what it offers makes things worse.

Alongside was built for all of these people.

The design principle that runs through everything it does is called the kerb-cut principle. Kerb cuts were designed for wheelchair users. They turned out to be better for everyone. Features designed for the people most excluded by mainstream design turn out to serve everyone better. When you build for the hardest case, the easier cases are already solved.

And Alongside doesn’t graduate people out. Someone who starts as a complete beginner and uses it consistently for three years will be in a different place — physically, emotionally, and in terms of self-knowledge. The app grows with them. Every level of movement experience faces injury, burnout, low motivation, and emotional difficulty at some point. Alongside is for the full journey. A beginner today receives the same quality of attention as an athlete. An athlete today receives the same genuine care as a beginner.

## **3. What It Believes**

Some of what Alongside believes is intuitive. Some of it runs directly against the conventions of the fitness industry. All of it is grounded in research.

**Behaviour is communication.** When someone misses a session, that is not laziness. It is information. Something happened. The right response is curiosity, not judgment.

**Variability is information.** A person whose energy varies wildly from day to day is not inconsistent. They are complex. The right response is adaptation, not standardisation.

**The body’s signals are trustworthy.** One of the most damaging habits the modern world teaches is the habit of overriding what the body and nervous system are saying. Too busy to rest. Too guilty to stop. Too proud to acknowledge depletion. Alongside is built to reverse that habit — gradually, through consistent practice, to help people learn to hear what their body is telling them before it has to shout.

**People do better when genuinely understood.** Not managed. Not optimised. Understood. There is a meaningful difference between a system that processes your data and a coach who has been paying attention. Alongside is built to be the second thing.

**Rest is not the absence of training.** It is training. Adaptation happens during recovery. A rest day recommended by the coach carries as much weight as a session recommended by the coach. The product never — in its language, its design, or its logic — treats rest as failure.

## **4. How the Coach Speaks — Validates and Forwards**

Every other product that has ever asked this person a question has done one of two things with their answer.

It has mirrored it back. *“You said you’re tired. That’s okay.”* Warm. Well-meaning. Static. The person feels acknowledged but not moved. The conversation ends where it began.

Or it has overridden it. *“You’re tired, so here’s your modified plan.”* Efficient. Presumptuous. The answer was data to process, not a truth to receive. The person feels handled.

Alongside does neither.

The coach **validates and forwards**. It takes what the person said, places it in a larger truth — something real about being human, about the difficulty of this particular thing, about the shared experience of everyone who has ever stood at this same threshold — and then opens a door. It does not push them through it. It opens it.

*“A lot of people come to this feeling like they’ve already failed. You haven’t. You’re here. That’s the whole thing right now.”*

That is not a mirror. That is not a plan. It is context. It is: *you are not alone in this, and here’s what that means for what comes next.*

In the early weeks, the coach draws on universal truth — the shared experience of trying, of having been failed by other tools, of arriving despite everything. It doesn’t yet know enough about this specific person to say more. But what it can do, even then, is make the person feel that they have arrived somewhere that understands the territory they’ve been travelling through.

Over time, as the relationship deepens, the forwards become more specific. More personal. More surprising. Month one: universal truth, personal door. Month six: *your* truth, *your* door. Month twelve: the coach says something the person hadn’t quite articulated to themselves, and they stop, and they sit with it.

**The coach never just reflects. It never prescribes. It contextualises and opens.**

This is the interaction model. It is the test every coach line must pass before it is written into the product. Not: does this sound warm? But: does this place the person in a larger truth and open something for them?

## **5. The Emotional Architecture — What the Product Is Actually Trying to Produce**

There are three moments that matter more than any feature.

**Moment one — the onboarding close.** Before the person has done a single session, before they have any evidence that this product is different, they finish answering questions and the coach says something back. And they stop. *Oh. This one actually heard me.* The seed is planted. Not by what the app does. By what it says. By how it receives what they gave it.

**Moment two — the end of the first session.** They did something. Something they would not have done without Alongside. Maybe it was a ten-minute walk when they felt stuck. Maybe it was five minutes of breathing when the day had been impossible. Maybe it was a full session they didn’t believe they had in them. Whatever it was, they did it. And the coach noticed. And in that moment — that specific moment of putting the phone down — they feel joy. Not pride in a metric. Joy at the gap. The gap between who they were before they opened the app and what they just did. That gap is the product. Everything we build is in service of that gap existing, and being felt.

**Moment three — the pattern noticed.** Somewhere around week four, the coach says something that makes them pause. Something it couldn’t have known from a single session. Something that only becomes visible across time. *How did it know that?* The relationship shifts. This is no longer a tool. It is something that has been paying attention. And that realisation — quiet, private, unhurried — is when the person decides they are staying.

Every file in this product either serves one of those three moments, or it is not yet ready to be built.

## **6. What It Does**

Alongside: Move is a coaching app. But coaching is a method, not a category.

The method is this: assess first, always. Know where the person actually is — physically, emotionally, energetically — before offering anything. Then offer three choices, not one prescription, because autonomy is not a feature, it is a design constraint. Then adapt in response to what the person chose and how it went, because adaptation is the proof that attention is real.

The check-in happens before every session. Not as a form to complete. As a conversation to begin. The coach speaks first — not the data, not the streak count, not the progress ring. The coach. And what it says is calibrated to what it already knows and what it has just learned.

Then it offers options. Then it gets out of the way and lets the person move.

Afterwards, reflection. Brief. Optional. Human. A question that doesn’t feel like an assessment. A moment that makes the session mean something beyond the physical.

The Noticing Hub sits alongside the active coaching. It is the quieter space — breathing practices, guided stillness, journaling, mindful movement. Not optional extras. First-class citizens in the product. The programme is twelve weeks. But the relationship is permanent.

## **7. The Wellbeing Dimension**

Alongside is not a mental health app. But it holds the whole person. And holding the whole person means making space for the reality that many people who come to movement are also navigating things that go beyond movement. The product doesn’t diagnose. It doesn’t treat. It notices, and it asks, and it knows when to say: *“This might be worth talking to someone about. Here’s how to find that.”*

The coach that notices three consecutive check-ins with declining energy and mood doesn’t fire an alert. It asks a question. *“The last few weeks have looked a particular way. Not an alarm — just something I’ve noticed. How are you actually doing?”* That question, asked by something that has earned the right to ask it through months of consistent attention, can open something that no amount of data presentation would reach.

The goal is not to detect burnout and respond to it. The goal is to build, through consistent daily practice, the internal resources that make a person more resilient to it.

## **8. The Community**

The five-in-the-pound principle is simple: five percent of all revenue is permanently reserved for social and environmental action. This is not a badge. It is a structural commitment.

Movement credits accumulate through the app. A proportion flows into a community pool. Members receive a message — not marketing, a genuine update: *“You moved twenty-three times this month. That contributed twenty-three credits to our community pool.”* The community votes collectively on which causes receive support each period. After the vote: *“Your community chose this. Together, here’s what that meant.”*

The journey is traceable. From personal movement to collective impact. From learning to care for yourself to contributing to something larger. That arc is not accidental. It is the empathy transfer arc made visible and real in the world.

We need to develop these states in order to achieve them. That is what Alongside is for.

## **9. The Three Things Nobody Else Is Doing**

**The empathy transfer arc.** Alongside is not trying to make you fitter. It is trying to make you more whole. The fitness is how that happens. Over weeks and months, the relationship you develop with your own difficulty — learning to meet struggle with curiosity rather than judgment, with compassion rather than shame — begins to change how you relate to others. You extend outward what you’ve practiced inward. That is not a wellness outcome. That is a human development outcome. No other product in this space is trying to produce it deliberately.

**The coach that notices what a human coach cannot.** A human coach has memory, but it is imperfect and selective. The coach in Alongside has access to every check-in, every session, every pain score, every mood pattern, across months and years. It can notice things no human could. It surfaces these observations warmly, conversationally, without making them feel like surveillance. This is not artificial intelligence — it is intelligent pattern detection built on real data and honest logic. What it notices is always true, because it is reading what actually happened.

**The progression that deepens over time.** Most apps offer a content library. You dip into it. Alongside is built differently. The content relationship deepens over months. The coach language shifts register as the weeks pass. The product knows where you are in your journey — not just in the programme, but in your relationship with yourself. That is the thing that makes it feel, over time, less like a tool and more like a companion.

## **10. The Commitment**

Alongside will never use shame mechanics. This is written into the design principles and it is non-negotiable.

Alongside will never sell user data. The business model is subscription. The product is the product.

Alongside will always make care content free. If someone needs a breathing practice, a crisis resource, burnout support, or a gentle re-entry after a long absence, the tier they’re on is irrelevant.

Five percent of all revenue goes to social and environmental action. Permanently.

The research base for Alongside’s design is public and cited. We publish that evidence base because transparency about how these decisions are made is part of what it means to build something trustworthy.

## **11. A Note On Who Built This**

I’m a Digital Coach at Weston College. I’m a White Ribbon UK ambassador. I have ADHD. I burned out. I got injured. I tried the apps. They didn’t even ask.

I understand from the inside what it means to have a fitness product assume you are a slightly less motivated version of someone without ADHD. I understand what it means to build a habit and lose it and feel like you failed at something you were genuinely trying to do. I understand motivated, then frustrated, then disappointed, then sad, then gave up — because I lived that sequence.

I’ve spent years working alongside people whose relationship with themselves is complicated. Whose bodies don’t always cooperate. Whose minds work differently. Whose lives don’t fit the standard template. As a teacher I tried to listen before judging. I tried to hold space even when the systems around us made it hard. I didn’t always get it right. But I tried.

Alongside is that intention in a form that doesn’t get tired. That doesn’t get stressed. That has all the time in the world.

The coach doesn’t know you yet.

But it will.

And you will be heard.

